          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The original “Peano (space-filling) curve” is a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjective"} surjective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \to I^2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+interval"} closed interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I = [0, 1]
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+space"} product
            with itself, the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square"} square
          %p
            One may characterize exactly which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+spaces"} Hausdorff spaces
            arise as the continuous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/images"} images
            of a unit interval. These are called
            = succeed "." do
              %em Peano spaces
          %p
            One can similarly show that there is a continuous surjection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^1 \to \mathbb{R}^2
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
            (both regarded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+spaces"} Euclidean spaces
            equipped with their
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+topology"} metric topology
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                \-Peano spaces
          %p
            Notice that, while of course there is also an injection
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}^2
            %em no
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
            between these two spaces, or generally between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+spaces"} Euclidean spaces
            of differing
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+invariance+of+dimension"} topological invariance of dimension
          %h2#construction Construction
          %p There are many constructions of space-filling curves, but one of the quickest is due to Lebesgue and is closely connected with the Cantor-Lebesgue function.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor+space"} Cantor space
            (following the “middle thirds” construction) can be described as the subspace
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1]
            consisting of points whose base-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            representation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo .
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 3
                  %mi …
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mn ∞
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 3
                        %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} .a_1 a_2 a_3 \ldots = \sum_{i=1}^{\infty} \frac{a_i}{3^i}
            has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i \in \{0, 2\}
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            (no
            = succeed "'s)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: C \to I
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mn ∞
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mn 3
                          %mi i
                    %mo )
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mn ∞
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi\left(\sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac{a_i}{3^i}\right) = \sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac{a_i/2}{2^i}
          %p
            (i.e., replace
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            representation by
            = succeed "\u2019s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            maps the two endpoints of any one of the open intervals removed during the middle-thirds construction to the same point, e.g., for the first middle third
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 2
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\frac1{3}, \frac{2}{3})
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn .02222222
                  %msub
                    %mi …
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn .01111111
                  %msub
                    %mi …
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mn 10000000
                  %msub
                    %mi …
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn .20000000
                  %msub
                    %mi …
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(.02222222\ldots_3) = .01111111\ldots_2 = 10000000\ldots_2 = \phi(.20000000\ldots_3).
          %p
            In any case, it is very easy to see that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: C \to I
            is a continuous surjective map.
          %p
            Now:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is homeomorphic to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+space"} product space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^\mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 = \{0, 1\}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo +
                    %mi ℕ
