            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \to I
            by linear interpolation, we get the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantor_function"} Cantor-Lebesgue function
          %h2#hahnmazurkiewicz_theorem Hahn-Mazurkiewicz theorem
          %p The eponymous theorem may be stated as follows:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              admits a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: I \to X
              from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+interval"} closed interval
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              if and only if it is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} connected, locally connected
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metrizable+space"} metrizable space
          %p
            (N.B. According to the nLab, connected spaces are
            = succeed "!)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} nonempty
          %p
            The “only if” half is relatively easy; see
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/connected+space#arcconnectedness"} here
            for some details. The “if” half is rather more involved, but
            %a{:href => "https://www.amazon.com/dp/0486434796/?tag=stackoverfl08-20"} Willard’s General Topology
            contains a proof. A space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            satisfying the stated conditions is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Peano space
          %p
            Given this characterization, it is not difficult to characterize which spaces are continuous images of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path-connected+space"} path-connected
              Hausdorff space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              admits a continuous surjection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \to X
              if and only if it is a
              = succeed "-Peano" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo :
                        %mi ℕ
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup_{n: \mathbb{N}} A_n
              of Peano spaces.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              The “only if” half being fairly obvious, the “if” part may be proved as follows. Since there are continuous surjections
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, \infty) \to \mathbb{R}
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \to [0, \infty)
              = succeed "-Peano" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, \infty)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              choose a continuous surjection
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi n
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_n: [2 n, 2 n + 1] \to A_n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              choose a path
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mi n
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_n: [2 n + 1, 2 n + 2] \to X
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_n(2 n + 1) = f_n(2 n + 1)
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_n(2 n + 2) = f_{n+1}(2 n + 2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_n
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_n
              paste together to form a continuous surjection
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, \infty) \to X
          %p
            An example of such a space is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Warsaw+circle"} Warsaw circle
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+invariance+of+dimension"} topological invariance of dimension
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} connected space
          %h2#references References
          %p The original article:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giuseppe+Peano"}> Giuseppe Peano
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Sur une courbe, qui remplit toute une aire plane
              %strong 36
              1 (1890) 157–160 [
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01199438"}> doi:10.1007/BF01199438
              ]
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peano_curve"} Peano curve
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  Hans Sagan
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Hans+Sagan"} ?
                ,
                %em Space-filling curves
                (1994) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4612-0871-6"}> doi:10.1007/978-1-4612-0871-6
                ]
          %p The proof of the Hahn-Mazurkiewicz theorem is given in section 31 (page 219ff) within chapter 8 of Willard’s classic text:
          %ul
            %li
              Stephen Willard,
              %em General Topology
              (Dover Edition 2004). Originally published by Addison-Wesley, 1970. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.amazon.com/dp/0486434796/?tag=stackoverfl08-20"}> link to vendor
              )
          %p The question of which spaces are continuous images of the real line was asked (and answered with dispatch by Jeff Strom) at MathOverflow:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"} Todd Trimble
              (https://mathoverflow.net/users/2926/todd-trimble), continuous images of open intervals, URL (version: 2014-05-25):
              %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/questions/168084/continuous-images-of-open-intervals"} https://mathoverflow.net/q/168084
        .revisedby
          %p
